      "sample_index": 0,
      "text": "To determine the domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\), we need to identify the values of \\( x \\) for which the function is defined. The function involves a square root in the denominator, so we must ensure that the expression inside the square root is positive and that the denominator is not zero.\n\n1. **Identify the domain condition:**\n   The expression inside the square root is \\( x \\). For the square root to be defined, the argument must be non-negative:\n   \\[\n   x > 0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Combine the conditions:**\n   The domain of the function \\( u(x) \\) is all positive real numbers. In interval notation, this is written as:\n   \\[\n   (0, \\infty)\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the domain of the function \\( u(x) = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\) is \\(\\boxed{(0, \\infty)}\\).",
